The Room is an excellent puzzle game built around (somewhat misappropriately named) unlocking a box (which advances you to new rooms and new boxes, so I guess
). The puzzles were very tight, focused and clever. When I was challenged on particularly difficult steps along the way, it was because I missed something (often quite obvious in hindsight) and never felt like I was stumped because was the logic that was non-sequiter or that I needed to engage in Adventure game style"pixel hunting" (more like randomly clicking in various areas in the image in this HD era) to solve the puzzles.
If I had to level any criticism against the game, it is very short. I plowed through the entire game in around two hours, with hints turned off and without using any guides. The price of the title, however, reflects the short play time, high quality visuals, clever puzzles and excellent sound design. I picked it up for a pittance on a Steam sale and would easily recommend anyone purchase it at that price. Even at full price, I would still recommend it if you're in the market for a clever, logic based puzzle game.

I pre-ordered BL2 back in the days this wasn't a bad thing. I loved it from the first moment I played it, and I still do.
Years of grinding with several characters, hoarding ridiculous amounts of weapons and shields, killing way too many living beings and destroying robots. And I still like it.
If you like shooters, but still want something else than CoD;
If you like first person RPG's, but also like fast-paced gameplay;
If you like dark humour;
Just try this game.
If you would like to know more about this game, I would advise you to look for the other reviews.

Duet is a minimalist game that will test your reflexes. You may think it'll be a peaceful experience with the given name but... it's hard.
You control a ring with adherent blue and red balls and you have to dodge obstacles. That may not sound hard, but you can't control the balls, all you can do is rotating (
). Not all obstacles are just static blocks, but they can also rotate, move, or be invisible for few seconds.
The game has a pretty good feel with the atmosphere built of minimalistic design and trance music. You just have to take a patient step towards the levels full of possible anger. Anyway, it's good to mention the developers, with the difficulty in mind, made levels shorter. Expect levels usually shorter than 30 seconds, so losing near the finish line is not so much stressful (not like in
).
Duet, with consider for the lower price, is a nice game with overall polished design that's worth recommending to anybody who want a challenge. Great mobile port!

